codex
The next residual exposes a semantic typing limitation: a Boolean “all elements are ints” predicate does not refine a symbolic `Val` into K’s `Int` subsort. I’m repairing this at the source level with an explicit `isinstance(value, int)` guard, which the supplied semantics itself case-splits by value sort. This preserves all contract-valid integer inputs and lets non-integer modeled values be ignored rather than stuck.
apply patch
patch: completed
/workspace/solution.py
/workspace/spec.k
/workspace/verification.k
diff --git a/solution.py b/solution.py
new file mode 100644
index 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000..9080fc032fe388a83ac293a12186de03c312f728
--- /dev/null
+++ b/solution.py
@@ -0,0 +1,19 @@
+def largest_smallest_integers(lst):
+    largest_negative = 0
+    smallest_positive = 0
+    value = 0
+
+    for value in lst:
+        if isinstance(value, int):
+            if value < 0:
+                if largest_negative == 0 or value > largest_negative:
+                    largest_negative = value
+
+            if value > 0:
+                if smallest_positive == 0 or value < smallest_positive:
+                    smallest_positive = value
+
+    return (
+        None if largest_negative == 0 else largest_negative,
+        None if smallest_positive == 0 else smallest_positive,
+    )
